WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.600
- You guys have turned the medical

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.980
reaction series into a total hit.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.040
But I wanted to take
things to the next level,

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.140
so I partnered with the
folks from the TV show

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.440
The Resident to give
you a behind-the-scenes

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.630
look of the set,

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.150
interview one of the main actors

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.020
from the show, Manish Dayal,

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.100
and even tried my hand at acting.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.037
They offered me an Emmy, I was like,

00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:20.177
"Chill for a second."

00:00:21.325 --> 00:00:24.325
(slow techno music)

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.790
I'm here with Manish Dayal,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.000
who plays Dr. Devon Pravesh.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.930
- Amazing doctor, gifted
doctor, five stars.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.430
- Do you ever have difficulty on set?

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.724
There's a word you don't know,
how do you prepare for that?

00:00:40.724 --> 00:00:42.020
- Oh, all the time.
(Mike laughs)

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.750
I mean, every script,
there are these words.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.130
I often will look at the procedure

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.080
or what the word means on YouTube first,

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.870
and then I have sort of a visual.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:50.910
There's one in particular

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.100
that really threw us last
year was naegleria fowleri.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.360
- Naegleria fowleri.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.900
- That's right.
- What's your view

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.640
or how has your view changed

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.290
of doctors now playing a doctor?

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.600
- Oh, I've always had
great respect for doctors.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.350
The dedication, the commitment.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.510
I have an even greater
respect for just the amount

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.890
of knowledge that doctors have

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.490
to retain and exercise every day.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.250
It's immense.
- It's crazy,

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.320
because on top of having to know,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.620
even your character, well-researched,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.300
well-studied Harvard, knows a
lot of textbook information,

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.560
but then applying it in a practical sense

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.220
to another human being who's so complex,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.970
that's such a challenge that people

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:27.950
normally don't highlight.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.550
- There's a stage of
being a doctor in school

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.060
and outside of the hospital and then

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.820
you see doctors inside of the hospital

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.800
and what changes and how they're applying

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.220
textbook information,
academics, to practice

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.750
and it can be discombobulating.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.180
Especially for Devon in the first season.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.013
- Yeah, big time.

00:01:42.013 --> 00:01:44.510
- What is the first
question you ask in code?

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.820
- Rhythm.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:46.760
- We're on set right now.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:47.850
You have multiple sets here.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.570
You have the ER, OR.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.730
Do you mind giving us a little tour

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.730
so we can sort of break down

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.830
how realistic this is
from a medical standpoint?

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.000
- Absolutely, no problem at all.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.950
This right here is the ER.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.550
We have 10 trauma bays here.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:02.640
This is the nurses station.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.630
- Yup, very accurate, might I add.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.260
- Yeah, tell about what this is--

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.360
- Well, so initially
when I walked in here,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.500
I was really impressed with
the attention to detail.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.660
This is really cool to
show people at home,

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.870
the Post-its are legit.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.340
Our computers--
- Legit Post-its.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.173
- Legit Post-its.
- I didn't post this.

00:02:18.173 --> 00:02:20.520
- Our computers are covered in Post-its.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.930
- These are--
- Do the phones work?

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.070
How accurate--
- Oh, no, no.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.280
- Don't look at these.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.160
Patient privacy, Dane, what are you doing?

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.048
- Don't violate HIPAA, right?

00:02:27.048 --> 00:02:28.120
- (laughs) Yeah, HIPAA, see?

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:29.160
He knows about HIPAA.
- Yeah.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.410
- This is very accurate.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.560
The beds look like they're
functional, the rails go up.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:33.393
So this is--

00:02:33.393 --> 00:02:34.226
- Yes, so we--
- This is very realistic.

00:02:34.226 --> 00:02:35.380
- During a scene though dude,

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.603
so basically, we'll be
doing a scene and like--

00:02:36.603 --> 00:02:37.440
- (laughs) Figuring out how to lower it.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.120
- And then it doesn't work!

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.080
- This blood pressure
reading is a little crazy,

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.780
91 over 132 so this patient--

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.253
- What's wrong with that patient?

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.660
- This is not a live
person's blood pressure.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:49.748
- The lower number--
- This person's dead.

00:02:49.748 --> 00:02:51.060
- Yeah, this person's dead.
- This person is

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.160
absolutely dead.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.860
This is the ICU.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.250
We have a--
- This is very accurate

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.260
as well.
- Is it?

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.830
- Yeah, the rooms need to be
able to be isolation rooms.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.140
The way that they're spread
apart is very accurate.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.770
The nursing station being in the middle,

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.560
to have the view of all the
rooms is very important.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.660
This chart is actually
really realistic, also.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.910
Because when the patient is in the ICU,

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:16.330
they can develop something
known as delirium

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:17.590
where they don't know where they are

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.270
and looking at this can help them a lot.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.760
They'll know who their nurse
is, who their doctor is.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.020
It also is helpful to some
of the nurses taking care

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:25.960
of the patients to know what
they're supposed to be eating.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:28.010
Honestly, if I walked in here

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.800
and I had no idea where I was prior,

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.340
I would say, I'm definitely in a hospital.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.160
There's no way I would
say that this is a set.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:34.673
- Now, we're gonna move to the OR.

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:35.506
- Let's move to the OR.

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:37.990
- Which is where all the blood

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:39.060
and the guts and the gore...
(Mike laughs)

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.283
- [Dr. Mike] Which was that
first scene of the entire show.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.353
- That's where we're goin' right now.

00:03:45.353 --> 00:03:46.620
- That's when the mistake happened.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.170
I got chills during that part.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.500
- Really?
- 'Cause I've seen

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.290
medical errors happen in front of me.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.860
Maybe not to that extent,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.110
but I remember, it brought
up all these feelings

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.350
of what I felt during those moments.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.420
It made you feel as if you
were there, so it was--

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.540
- And especially as a
young doctor when you have,

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.810
when the responsibility to your job,

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.200
but also your bosses and
the people who employ you.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.690
- A lot of conflicting interests.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:09.523
Wow.

00:04:09.523 --> 00:04:11.430
- This is the ICU room.
- Hello!

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.110
- I mean, there's so much
blood on this floor usually.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:15.270
You wouldn't even be able to walk.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.040
We have this machine, the
blood-splatter machine.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.310
There's a pump that
looks like a helium pump.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.940
And attached to it is a clear tube

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.620
that gets hidden under the clothing,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.450
or taped behind the arm of the patient.

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.060
So when it's time for the blood to squirt,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.260
they pump it, and the blood

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.320
just squirts up.
- Squirts out.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.330
- And usually my character's

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:36.077
behind that glass, just
watching, 'cause I don't...

00:04:36.077 --> 00:04:37.596
- 'Cause you're not a surgical resident.

00:04:37.596 --> 00:04:38.990
- I'm not a surgeon, I wish!

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.277
- You do rotate, right?

00:04:41.175 --> 00:04:42.200
You have to learn experience?
- We do rotate, I mean,

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.010
right now, I'm in the ER and if I'm lucky,

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.140
I'll get to move to
surgery, but we'll see.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.219
This is another, here, we'll go in here.

00:04:48.219 --> 00:04:49.210
- Oh, wow.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.700
- In the next episode, which
we start shooting on Monday,

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.560
it's the Halloween episode.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.930
And everyone is tasked
with a different job.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.270
But there's this big Halloween party,

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:00.910
so the doctors all go
to the Halloween party.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.830
My character's left in the ER

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.781
to treat this arm that comes in.

00:05:05.781 --> 00:05:07.180
What up, what up!

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.700
- These lights are legitimate.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.860
You see this little covering on the light

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.880
is to make sure that
even when you touch it,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.970
you still maintain your sterility.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.717
Sunshine is the one that generally
gives you the guidance on

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:18.780
mistakes that you're making.
- Yes.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.000
She's our medical wizard, yeah.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.087
- How often is she saying,

00:05:22.087 --> 00:05:23.737
"Guys, you have to stop the scene

00:05:23.737 --> 00:05:25.540
"because the technique is wrong here."

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:26.373
Does that happen often?

00:05:26.373 --> 00:05:29.410
- Well, she won't stop
it while we're taking.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.590
She'll wait until we call cut--

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:32.830
- Got it.
- To come in with a note,

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:34.840
and we'll implement it and shoot it again.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.080
- Awesome, yeah, 'cause--
- Because there's always

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.070
pieces they can take.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.320
- How many cameras are going at--

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.140
- Three, and sometimes four.
- Wow.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.440
We watched them film a scene for Season 2,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.260
not gonna spill any
secrets but it's incredible

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.730
how many things are
happening simultaneously.

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:51.080
There's multiple cameras moving around,

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.510
actors, actresses, extras.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:56.440
It blew my mind how much needs to happen

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.350
in order for one scene to get taken

00:05:58.350 --> 00:05:59.240
in the perfect way.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:00.073
Who is this gentleman?

00:06:00.073 --> 00:06:03.270
- [Manish] This is, let's see here.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.230
I have no clue who this is.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.080
- And then this is the viewing room?

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.860
- There's a scene in the first episode

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.930
where we're all viewing a
very complicated surgery.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.550
If you're an internist or
someone not allowed in there...

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:17.680
- Medical students?
- Medical students can

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:18.860
hang out in here.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:20.450
- So medical students at home,

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:22.440
this is where you'd be
hanging out watching the

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:23.810
operation go live.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:28.000
The signs are accurate in
terms of which way to go.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.470
They're complicated just
like they are in real life.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:33.490
After getting the tour of the entire set,

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.830
I really wanted to know
firsthand what it would be like

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.440
to be an actor on a medical drama.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.970
Manish was kind enough to do a little

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.290
improv session with me.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.290
- Hi, I'm Dr. Pravesh.
What's your name?

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:45.900
- Hi, doctor. What took so long?

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:46.733
I don't understand.

00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:47.650
I've been here for an hour.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.250
- I'm sorry, you see all these
patients we have here now.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:51.370
What brings you into the ER today?

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:53.290
- My stomach's been killing me, doc.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:54.123
- Okay.
- I don't want

00:06:54.123 --> 00:06:57.500
to be frank here, but my
stool, it's disgusting.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.450
- Wow, how long have
you had these symptoms?

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:00.710
- Like, 24 hours.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:02.000
I came back from Mexico.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:02.930
I don't know if it's ZIKA.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:04.970
What am I doing here, doc?

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:06.651
Is this a, it's a tumor.

00:07:06.651 --> 00:07:08.030
- No, it's not a tumor.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:09.050
We'll get to the bottom of that.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.640
- Oh, my stomach. I like
your stethoscope, by the way.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:12.473
- Oh, thank you.

00:07:12.473 --> 00:07:13.670
- Matte black, it's a great choice.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:15.120
- It is, yeah.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.420
They sponsor my practice.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.150
- Doctor, why are you
listening to my lungs

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:21.381
when I told you my stomach hurts?

00:07:21.381 --> 00:07:22.247
(laughing)
- I don't understand.

00:07:22.247 --> 00:07:23.550
You see, I can't act.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:25.287
I'm sorry, I'm gonna
stick to being a doctor.

00:07:25.287 --> 00:07:26.620
All right, Manish, we appreciate you

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.350
taking out the time showing
us behind the scenes,

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:32.020
allowing me to show off
my horrible acting chops.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.350
What grade do you give me?

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:36.190
- I give you a solid A.
- Wow.

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:37.170
- That's too kind.
- Yes.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:38.140
- He's obviously lying,

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:39.900
but again thank you for
taking out the time.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.810
Everyone, check out the season premier

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.360
of Season 2 of The Resident.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.097
Stay happy and healthy.

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:48.097
(slow techno music)

